task.description:
refreshedTaskHead discards the outcome of the refresh it forced, so when that refresh fails the caller re-bases onto the very head the server just refused.

internal/webui/assets/index.html:1621-1625:

    async function refreshedTaskHead(taskID) {
      await currentRefreshResult(refresh());
      const refreshed = latestTasks.find((candidate) => candidate.id === taskID);
      return refreshed && refreshed.head || "";
    }

currentRefreshResult returns {status} of "applied" | "failed" | "superseded" and the return value is thrown away; latestTasks is then read unconditionally. refresh() swallows a fetch error into {status:"failed"} (index.html:1985) and leaves latestTasks at the last successful poll, so on a failed refresh the function hands back the pre-conflict head.

Two callers degrade:

1. drainIntents, index.html:1597. On a stale-write refusal it does `const rebased = await refreshedTaskHead(taskID); if (rebased) entry.head = rebased;`. If the forced GET /api/tasks fails (offline blip, server restart, `workbook serve` reloading), rebased is the refused head, entry.head is set right back to it, and every intent queued behind the conflict is refused in turn — the exact signature WB-01KZBT3J1AMW4E5F688WPNZGJZ was filed to remove. Reviewer reproduced this by cloning TestHandlerClientStaleWriteRollsBackRefreshesAndRebasesTheQueue with the post-conflict GET throwing: the sent heads were ["head-1","head-1"].

2. The detail form, index.html:1419. expectedHead is re-set to the refused head while the form tells the user "Your edits are kept; save again to apply them to the version the server holds", so the retry the message invites is refused identically.

Fix: honour the status. Something like `const result = await currentRefreshResult(refresh()); if (result.status !== "applied") return "";` so a failed refresh leaves the head alone, plus a way to surface the degraded state instead of silently inviting a doomed retry.

Deferred from PR #29 (merged as a2fd315) as a minor review finding. It is not a regression against pre-fix behavior — before that PR the head was never re-based at all — and it self-heals on the next successful one-second poll, so it did not block the merge. It is worth closing because it re-opens the fixed bug for the duration of any refresh outage.
